fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Here's our motive: we want to assign

to each \mathfrak {G}\colon \overline {\mathsf {Con}}, a set \mathfrak {G}{}^{\mathsf  A}

to each \mathfrak {h}\colon \overline {\mathsf {Sub}}\;\mathfrak {G}\;\mathfrak {H}, a function \mathfrak {h}{}^{\mathsf  A}\colon  \mathfrak {G}{}^{\mathsf  A}\to \mathfrak {H}{}^{\mathsf  A}

to each \mathcal {A}\colon \overline {\mathsf {Ty}}\;\mathfrak {G}, a family of sets \mathcal {A}{}^{\mathsf  A} \colon \mathfrak {G}{}^{\mathsf  A} \to  \mathsf {Set}

to each \mathrm {t}\colon  \overline {\mathsf {Tm}}\;\mathfrak {G}\;\mathcal {A}, a section \mathrm {t}{}^{\mathsf  A}\colon  \left \lparen \Gamma \colon \mathfrak {G}{}^{\mathsf  A}\right \rparen  \to  \mathcal {A}{}^{\mathsf  A}\;\Gamma  
The set \mathfrak {G}{}^{\mathsf  A} is what we refer to elsewhere as \mathfrak {G}{\operatorname -}\mathsf {Alg}; we just use the superscript notation here for uniformity. As will be the case with most \mathrm {oneGAT} inductions, the main content will be in defining (-){}^{\mathsf  A} for \mathsf {U}, \overline {\mathsf {El}}, \Pi , the de Bruijn indices, and for the @ operator; then the rest of the induction will more or less fall into place around it. For instance, we'll define \left \lparen \mathfrak {G}\rhd \mathcal {A}\right \rparen {}^{\mathsf  A} := \left \lparen \Gamma \colon \mathfrak {G}{}^{\mathsf  A}\right \rparen \times  \mathcal {A}{}^{\mathsf  A}\;\Gamma  that is: algebras for the GAT \mathfrak {G}\rhd  \mathcal {A} will be pairs (\Gamma ,x) where \Gamma  is a \mathfrak {G}-algebra and x\colon \mathcal {A}{}^{\mathsf  A}\;\Gamma .

